WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.880
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.560
Today we are going take a&nbsp;
collection of photos like these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.560
and magically, create a video where&nbsp;
we can fly through these photos.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:21.200
How is this even possible? Especially that&nbsp;
the input is only a handful of photos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.440
Well, we give it to a learning algorithm&nbsp;
and ask it to synthesize a photorealistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:33.200
video where we fly through the scene&nbsp;
as we please. That sounds impossible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:40.080
Especially that some information is given&nbsp;
about the scene, but this is really not much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:47.120
And everything in between these photos has&nbsp;
to be synthesized here. Let’s see how well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.880
this new method can perform that,&nbsp;
but don’t expect too much…and….wow.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:01.280
It took a handful of photos and filled in the&nbsp;
rest so well that we got a smooth and creamy video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:08.960
out of it. So, the images certainly look good in&nbsp;
isolation. Now let’s compare it to the real world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:15.600
images that we already have, but have hidden from&nbsp;
the algorithm, and hold on to your papers…wow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.040
this is breathtaking. It is not a great&nbsp;
deal different, is it? Does this mean…yes!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.640
It means that it guesses what reality&nbsp;
should look like almost perfectly.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:35.680
Now note that this mainly applies for looking&nbsp;
at these images in isolation. As soon as we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:41.120
weave them together into a video and start flying&nbsp;
through the scene, we will see some flickering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:48.960
artifacts, but that is to be expected. The AI&nbsp;
has to create so much information from so little,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:55.680
and the tiny inaccuracies that appear in each&nbsp;
image are different, and when played abruptly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:02.560
after each other, this introduces these artifacts.&nbsp;
So, which regions should we look at to find these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:08.800
flaws? Well, usually regions where we have&nbsp;
very little information in our set of photos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:15.440
and a lot of variation when we move our head.&nbsp;
For instance, visibility around thin structures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:21.840
is still a challenge. But of course, you know the&nbsp;
joke - how do you spot a Two Minute Papers viewer?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:28.560
They are always looking behind thin&nbsp;
fences. Shiny surfaces are a challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:34.000
too as they reflect their environment and&nbsp;
change a lot as we move our head around.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:41.360
So how does it compare to previous methods?&nbsp;
Well, it creates images that are sharper and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:49.200
more true to the real images. Look! What you see&nbsp;
here is a very rare sight. Usually, when we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:57.200
a new technique like this emerge, it almost always&nbsp;
does better on some datasets, and worse on others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:04.800
The comparisons are almost always a wash. But&nbsp;
here? Not at all. Not in the slightest. Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:11.920
here you see four previous techniques, four&nbsp;
scenes, and three different ways of measuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:18.240
the quality of the output images. And almost&nbsp;
none of it matters, because the new technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:25.520
reliably outperforms all of them, everywhere.&nbsp;
Except here, in this one case, depending on how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:32.480
we measure how good a solution is. And even&nbsp;
then, it’s quite close. Absolutely amazing.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:37.840
Make sure to also have a look at the paper in&nbsp;
the video description to see that it can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:43.520
perform filmic tone mapping, change the&nbsp;
exposure of the output images, and more.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:50.240
So, how did they pull this off? What hardware&nbsp;
do we need to train such a neural network?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:55.120
Do we need the server warehouses of&nbsp;
Google or OpenAI to make this happen?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.720
No, not at all! And here comes the best part…if&nbsp;
you have been holding on to your papers so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:10.880
now, squeeze that paper, because all it takes is&nbsp;
a consumer graphics card and 12 to 24 hours of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:18.080
training. And after that, we can use the neural&nbsp;
network for as long as we wish. So, recreating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:24.720
reality from a handful of photos with a neural&nbsp;
network that some people today can train at home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:31.680
themselves? The pace of progress in AI research&nbsp;
is absolutely amazing. What a time to be alive!

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.000
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

